[2026-05-12T21:51:44.027Z] Assistant message
1. High — `src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:561-574`, compared with stock `pipecat-core-code/src/pipecat/processors/aggregators/llm_response_universal.py:1252-1256,1421-1429`. Queued-not-started stale ids are only given `FunctionCallCancelFrame`s, but stock `LLMAssistantAggregator` only clears cancellations when `_function_calls_in_progress[id]` holds a real `FunctionCallInProgressFrame`; the `None` placeholders seeded by `FunctionCallsStartedFrame` are ignored. So the tests at `tests/test_nemotron_omni_aligned.py:805-935` overstate the guarantee: queued superseded ids can remain stuck in Pipecat bookkeeping even though the cancel frames/events were emitted.

2. High — `src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:372-383,472-506,1318-1363,1452-1459`. On newer-user-turn supersession, the code pops the batch state and resets per-turn dedup before it awaits cancellation of the running tool. If that tool finishes in the gap, it can still emit a stale `FunctionCallResultFrame` into the superseding turn and repopulate `_turn_tool_results`; if `_cancel_running_sync_function_calls()` reaches the finished task before the runner drops it, the same `tool_call_id` can then also get a cancel. This is the race where a superseded running call can produce stale result state or two terminal signals.

3. High — `src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1169-1208,392-396`. `_emit_round_limit_results()` is outside the batch state machine. If an interruption lands mid-broadcast, there is no `_current_batch_id`/`_tool_batch_states` entry to finish or cancel the remaining synthetic ids, so later requested `tool_call_id`s can be orphaned with neither result nor cancel. The happy-path test at `tests/test_nemotron_omni_aligned.py:1015-1081` does not cover this.

4. Medium — `src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:363-385,454-458,934-936`. The frame-time snapshot contract is broken and doubled. `process_frame()` computes the turn key from one deep-copied/normalized snapshot, then the background task later deep-copies and normalizes the live `LLMContext` again. That is the perf hit you called out, and it is also a correctness bug: reset/supersession decisions can be made against an earlier context version than the actual outbound request if shared context mutates between those two points.

5. Medium — `src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:439-458,364-377`. `_current_turn_user_key` is just the serialized latest user message. Two distinct top-level turns with identical user rows are treated as the same turn, so dedup/round state can bleed across turns, and an in-flight sync batch from the first turn will not be superseded/cancelled when the second identical turn arrives.

6. Low — `tests/test_nemotron_omni_aligned.py:1186-1260,1262-1320`. The routing tests do not prove the real bot topology. One calls `ParallelPipeline._parallel_push_frame()` directly; the other hand-wires stub passthrough processors and a dummy assistant instead of the real `NemotronMagpieWebSocketTTSService`/transport path. They are useful smoke tests, but they do not actually guarantee deployed re-entry routing.

Verified correct:
- `src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1284-1312` correctly keeps the sequential runner alive when a running sync tool is cancelled; the `CancelledError` is swallowed there and `_function_call_tasks.pop(task, None)` avoids the stock `del`/`KeyError` problem.
- `src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1286-1293` correctly prevents a stale queued item from dispatching `_run_function_call()` once its batch is marked stale.
- `src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:957-960,1160-1165` keeps `FunctionCallFromLLM.context` live; it passes the shared `LLMContext`, not the deep-copied request snapshot.
- No dangling references to the deleted cache/internal-loop helpers showed up in `src/` or `tests`, and `src/nemotron_voice/bot.py:426-436` matches the new `NemotronOmniAudioLLMService` constructor shape.
- I do not see a runner/generation deadlock in the intended path: the generation task only enqueues sync tools and returns, while the final tool waits for that task to finish before followup.

